QUESTIONS = [
    {"id": "q-topology", "at": {"node": "intro"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": "Does the bulk path integral include other geometries or topologies?",
     "a": r"Not for these models on $\mathbb R^d$. The paper finds only continuous field configurations, metric fluctuations included, on one fixed AdS space, and no sign of a sum over topologies. This is expected to change on spaces with circles, where vector models have large-$N$ phase transitions thought to map to changes of bulk topology; there the construction would need $U(N)$ gauge fields and holonomies, which the paper does not treat.",
     "answeredBy": ["intro-p3", "section-1.1-p2", "sec-local_to_bilocal-p3"]},
    {"id": "q-not-shown", "at": {"node": "section-1.1"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": "What does the paper not show?",
     "a": r"It does not prove that loops cancel to all orders, nor that its classical theory is Vasiliev's in some gauge. It does not write the finite-$N$ constraints in bulk terms, or check that the action is local at distances much larger than the AdS radius. It treats only Euclidean $\mathbb R^d$, so backgrounds with circles, Lorentzian signature, fermions and matrix models are left open, as is the $O(N)$ ghost mismatch.",
     "answeredBy": ["section-2.3.3-p2", "section-1.1-p2", "section-1.1-p3"]},
    {"id": "q-notation", "at": {"node": "sec-local_to_bilocal"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": r"What do $N$, $V$, $d$, $\Delta_0$, $\Delta$ and $J$ stand for, and in which order are the limits taken?",
     "a": r"$N$ counts the scalars and $d>2$ is the boundary dimension, so the bulk is AdS$_{d+1}$. $V$ is the number of lattice points of the regularized theory, standing for UV and IR cutoffs. $\Delta_0=(d-2)/2$ is the free scalar's dimension, and $\Delta$, $J$ label conformal modes and bulk fields, with $\Delta=\frac d2+is$ on the principal series. The $1/N$ expansion takes $N\to\infty$ first, so $N\ge V$, and removes the cutoff afterwards; finite $N$ in the continuum means $N<V$.",
     "answeredBy": ["sec-local_to_bilocal-p5", "section-2.1-p4", "sec-pert_bilocal-p1"]},
    {"id": "q-finite-n", "at": {"node": "sec-local_to_bilocal"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": r"What happens at finite $N$?",
     "a": r"Then $N<V$, and $G$ has rank at most $N$, which relates products of $N+1$ of its components. A path integral over the allowed $G$ still exists, but its explicit form is not known. Mapped to the bulk, the relations become non-local constraints on products of bulk fields, which exclude most configurations, empty AdS among them. The paper expects the passage from $N\ge V$ to $N<V$ to be smooth but does not show it.",
     "answeredBy": ["sec-local_to_bilocal-p5", "sec-local_to_bilocal-p6", "section-2.1-p5", "section-5.3-p4"]},
    {"id": "q-loops", "at": {"node": "sec-bilocal_1_loop"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": "Why must loop corrections cancel, and is this proven?",
     "a": r"In the free theory every connected correlator of $\sqrt N\eta$ comes from a single loop of scalar propagators and is exactly proportional to $N$, so in the $1/N$ expansion every loop correction must vanish, with the counter-terms supplying the cancellation. The paper verifies this at one loop, in the bi-local theory and in the bulk, and reduces every diagram to powers of $V$ times a tree, but gives no explicit all-orders proof; it follows implicitly from the exact change of variables.",
     "answeredBy": ["sec-bilocal_1_loop-p1", "section-2.3.3-p2", "section-6.3-p4"]},
    {"id": "q-dimensions", "at": {"node": "sec-conformal_basis"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": r"Why are $d<4$, $d=4$ and $d=2$ treated differently?",
     "a": r"The coefficients $C_{\Delta,J}$ are defined by an integral that converges only for $\Delta_0>d/4$, that is $d>4$ for free fields. For $2<d<4$ the paper expands the double shadow of $\eta$ instead and deforms the $J=0$ contour to include $\Delta=d-2$ and exclude its shadow 2, which turns the scalar maps into differential operators. At $d=4$ the change of variables breaks down, and results are continued from $d>4$. In $d=2$ the free propagator is a logarithm and that case is not considered.",
     "answeredBy": ["sec-conformal_basis-p7", "sec-conformal_basis-p9", "sec-conformal_basis-p11", "sec-pert_bilocal-p1"]},
    {"id": "q-unique", "at": {"node": "sec-cft_to_ads"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": "Is the map from the CFT to the bulk unique?",
     "a": r"No. Conformal symmetry fixes it only up to a factor $f_{\Delta,J}$ for each mode. Consistency with the shadow relation fixes the ratio $f_{\Delta,J}/f_{d-\Delta,J}$, reality fixes its phase, and finiteness needs $|f|$ to grow at most like a power along the principal series. The rest is a choice of bulk field variables: one choice makes the quadratic action local, and bulk diagrams depend on $f$ only through their external legs.",
     "answeredBy": ["sec-cft_to_ads-p1", "sec-cft_to_ads-p2", "section-5.1-p3", "section-6.3-p2"]},
    {"id": "q-hkll", "at": {"node": "sec-cft_to_ads"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": "How does this map differ from the usual reconstruction of bulk fields?",
     "a": r"HKLL-type reconstruction writes on-shell bulk fields as smeared local CFT operators. This map is off-shell and exact: it sends the bi-local field, a variable of the CFT path integral, to bulk field configurations, so it maps one path integral to another, actions included. Only in the boundary limit, and on-shell, does it reduce to the familiar dictionary between $\Phi_J$ and the spin-$J$ single-trace operator.",
     "answeredBy": ["footnotes", "sec-single_trace_dual-p2"]},
    {"id": "q-local", "at": {"node": "sec-quadratic_action"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": "Is the bulk theory local?",
     "a": r"Only in part. With the special normalization $f^{\rm local}$ the quadratic action is local, but it has four derivatives, which is why each spin carries two particles. The cubic and higher vertices are non-local for any choice of $f$. On general grounds the dual of a free theory is not expected to be local below the AdS radius, but it should be at much longer distances; whether this action has that property is left open.",
     "answeredBy": ["section-5.1-p2", "sec-bulk_theory-p1", "section-1.1-p3"]},
    {"id": "q-vasiliev", "at": {"node": "section-5.3"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": "Is this theory Vasiliev's high-spin gravity?",
     "a": r"The paper believes so but does not show it. For $U(N)$ the physical spectrum matches Vasiliev's, and Vasiliev's equations can be written on a fixed AdS background, as here. The paper expects a specific gauge fixing to reduce them to its action's classical equations, which would make the action the off-shell, loop-level definition of Vasiliev's theory. Its quadratic terms differ from the naive ones of Vasiliev's fields, and for $O(N)$ the ghost spins disagree.",
     "answeredBy": ["intro-p4", "section-5.3-p2", "section-5.3-p4"]},
    {"id": "q-ghosts", "at": {"node": "section-5.3"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": "Are the wrong-sign particles a problem?",
     "a": r"They are interpreted as unphysical. For $U(N)$ their spins and masses are those of the Faddeev–Popov ghosts of massless high-spin fields in a transverse traceless gauge, and they are subleading when a point goes to the boundary, so they do not affect CFT correlators. For $O(N)$ their spins disagree with those of Vasiliev's ghosts, which the paper cannot explain. Their role in a Lorentzian bulk Hilbert space is listed as an open question.",
     "answeredBy": ["section-5.3-p1", "section-5.3-p2", "section-5.3-p3", "section-1.1-p3"]},
    {"id": "q-cutoff", "at": {"node": "section-6.3"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": r"Where does the cutoff $V$ come from in the bulk?",
     "a": r"In the bi-local theory $V={\rm Tr}\,1$ is the number of lattice points; it enters the Jacobian and hence the counter-terms. In the bulk the same factor appears when a loop closes the completeness relation onto itself, giving $\int dP\,\delta(0)$. A radial cutoff $z>\varepsilon$ and a cutoff $|x|<L$ regularize it to $V\sim L^d/\varepsilon^d$, provided the sum over internal spins is done before the cutoffs are removed.",
     "answeredBy": ["section-2.1-p3", "app-bulk_dive-p1", "section-6.3-p5"]},
    {"id": "q-mass", "at": {"node": "sec-massDef"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": "What bulk configuration does the mass deformation give?",
     "a": r"The space stays AdS; at leading order in $1/N$ only the bulk scalar acquires an expectation value, which depends on $z$ through $mz$ alone and so breaks the AdS isometries. For small mass it is proportional to $m^2z^2$, the fall-off of the shadow of the mass operator. For finite mass in $d=3$ it is a contour integral evaluated numerically; the paper suggests comparing it with mass-deformed solutions of Vasiliev's equations.",
     "answeredBy": ["intro-p9", "sec-smallmass-p2", "sec-finmass-p2"]},
    {"id": "q-critical", "at": {"node": "critUNbulk"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": "Why does the critical model differ from the free one only by a boundary condition?",
     "a": r"At large $N$ the double-trace interaction changes only the two-point function of $\eta(x,x)$, which for $d<4$ is, off-shell, the boundary limit of the bulk scalar. In the bulk this replaces the physical scalar propagator $\Pi^{TT}_{d-2,0}$ by $\Pi^{TT}_{2,0}$, which solves the same equation with the other fall-off. Since the difference has no short-distance singularities, an argument of Giombi and Yin then gives the critical correlators at all orders, with the action unchanged.",
     "answeredBy": ["critUNbulk-p1", "critUNbulk-p2", "critUNbulk-p3"]},
]

BACKGROUND = [
    {"id": "bg-hs", "after": "intro-p3", "title": "Vector models and high-spin gravity", "audience": ["physicist", "student"],
     "body": r"""<p>A vector model of $N$ free scalars, restricted to $U(N)$ or $O(N)$ singlets, has single-trace operators $\phi^*\partial^J\phi$ of every spin $J$, and for $J\ge1$ they are conserved currents of dimension $d-2+J$. In AdS/CFT a conserved current of spin $J$ is dual to a massless gauge field of spin $J$, as the stress tensor is dual to the graviton. The bulk dual must therefore contain massless fields of all spins: a high-spin gravity, weakly coupled when $N$ is large, with $1/N$ playing the role of Newton's constant. Klebanov and Polyakov conjectured such a duality for the critical $O(N)$ model, and the free model is its simplest case. Vasiliev constructed consistent classical equations of motion for interacting massless fields of all spins in AdS, with an infinite-dimensional gauge symmetry, but no action principle is known for them, so neither loop corrections nor the relative weight of different solutions can be computed. The paper builds the bulk theory from the CFT side instead.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-harmonic", "after": "sec-conformal_basis-p1", "title": "Harmonic analysis on the conformal group", "audience": ["physicist", "student"],
     "body": r"""<p>As a function on a line expands in plane waves, a function of two points expands in irreducible representations of the conformal group, here the Euclidean group $SO(d+1,1)$. This harmonic analysis uses the principal series: a spin $J$ and a dimension $\Delta=\frac d2+is$ with real $s$, which are unitary representations although $\Delta$ is complex. The Clebsch–Gordan coefficients that project a product of two scalars onto one representation are conformal three-point functions, so a bi-local function becomes $\sum_J\int ds\int d^dy\,C_{\Delta,J}(y)\langle\mathcal O\mathcal O\mathcal O_{\Delta,J}(y)\rangle$. Representations with $\Delta$ and $d-\Delta$ are equivalent, related by the shadow transform, so only half of the $s$ line is independent. Physical operators, with real dimensions, do not lie on the contour: they appear as poles of the coefficients, picked up when the $s$ integral is closed, which is how a conformal partial wave expansion of a four-point function reproduces its conformal blocks. The paper treats the coefficients $C_{\Delta,J}(y)$ as the dynamical variables.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-quantization", "after": "sec-bulk_bc-p3", "title": "Two boundary conditions for a scalar in AdS", "audience": ["physicist", "student"],
     "body": r"""<p>A scalar of mass $m^2=\Delta(\Delta-d)$ in AdS$_{d+1}$ behaves near the boundary $z\to0$ as $z^{\Delta}$ or as $z^{d-\Delta}$; the two roots $\Delta_\pm$ are shadows of each other. Usually the slower fall-off is the source and the faster one the expectation value of an operator of dimension $\Delta_+$. When $\frac d2-1<\Delta_-<\frac d2$ both modes are normalizable, and one may instead impose the opposite boundary condition, with a dual operator of dimension $\Delta_-$ (Klebanov and Witten). The two choices are dual to two CFTs related by a double-trace flow: deforming the $\Delta_-$ theory by the square of its operator flows in the infrared to the $\Delta_+$ theory, at leading order in $1/N$. For vector models in $2<d<4$ the free theory's $\phi^*\phi$ has $\Delta_-=d-2$ and the critical theory's replacement has $\Delta_+=2$. The $z^{d-2}$ mode that the deformed contour adds to the mapped scalar is this alternative boundary condition.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-ghosts", "after": "section-5.3-p1", "title": "Massless high-spin fields, gauge fixing and ghosts", "audience": ["physicist", "student"],
     "body": r"""<p>A massless high-spin field of spin $J$ is described, following Fronsdal, by a symmetric tensor $\Phi_{\mu_1\dots\mu_J}$ with vanishing double trace and a gauge symmetry $\delta\Phi=\nabla_{(\mu_1}\xi_{\mu_2\dots\mu_J)}$ whose parameter is a traceless tensor of spin $J-1$, generalizing the photon and the graviton. Gauge fixing to a transverse traceless field, like the Lorenz or de Donder gauge, leaves only the physical polarizations, but the Faddeev–Popov determinant then contributes a ghost field of spin $J-1$ with a definite mass. In AdS a massless spin-$J$ field is dual to a conserved current of dimension $d-2+J$, and its ghost has spin $J-1$ and dimension $d+J-1$, the quantum numbers of the current's divergence. In one-loop computations the ghosts remove the unphysical gauge modes. The paper's wrong-sign modes, of spin $J'$ and dimension $d+J'$, have exactly these ghost quantum numbers with $J'=J-1$.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-critical", "after": "critUN-p1", "title": "The critical vector model at large N", "audience": ["student"],
     "body": r"""<p>Adding $\lambda(\sum_I|\phi_I|^2)^2$ to $N$ free scalars in $2<d<4$ is a relevant deformation: $\lambda$ has mass dimension $4-d$, so it grows towards the infrared, and the theory flows to an interacting fixed point, the critical vector model (for $O(N)$ with $N=1$ in $d=3$, the Ising transition). At large $N$ it is solvable: an auxiliary field $\sigma$ coupled to $\sum_I|\phi_I|^2$ resums the bubble diagrams into a geometric series, and at the fixed point $\sigma$ has dimension $2+O(1/N)$, while $\sum_I|\phi_I|^2$ itself, of dimension $d-2$ in the free theory, disappears from the spectrum. The other single-trace operators, the currents of spin $J>0$, keep their free dimensions at leading order and are broken only at order $1/N$. This is why, at leading order, the critical model differs from the free one only in its scalar, and why its bulk dual can differ only in the scalar's boundary condition.</p>"""},
]
